\DOC X86_STEPS_TAC

\TYPE {X86_STEPS_TAC : thm * (thm option array) -> int list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes a range of x86 machine instructions on a goal of the form
{A ?- eventually x86 P s}, advancing the state variable and replacing the
state-component assumptions with ones describing the new state.

\DESCRIBE
{th : thm * (thm option array)} is the pre-computed execution data for the
program being verified: a theorem giving the length of the machine-code list,
paired with a cache array of decoded instructions. {snums : int list} is the
list of step numbers to execute, typically written as a range; each number
names the intermediate state produced by the corresponding step.
{
      A ?- eventually x86 P s
     ========================  X86_STEPS_TAC th snums
      A' ?- eventually x86 P s'
}
\noindent Here {s'} is the state named by the last requested step number, and
the conclusion is unchanged apart from the state argument of {eventually}. In
the assumptions, those mentioning the old state (register and flag reads,
memory reads, the {bytes_loaded} assumption, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
relation) are discharged and replaced by corresponding assumptions about the
new state, with register/flag/memory values updated to the symbolic results of
the executed instructions, plus a new {MAYCHANGE} assumption relating the
original state to the new one; read assumptions for components written by the
executed instructions are added. Assumptions not referring to the state are
generally retained, though they may be removed and re-added and hence
reordered. When the requested range is empty or no step applies, the goal is
returned unchanged.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal, state-advancing behaviour,
always on goals whose conclusion is an {eventually x86} statement.

\ENDDOC
